--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Why GPL-3 rather than a permissive licence
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

megalooper is written against BBCut, and two of its classes subclass BBCut
classes directly:

    classes/LooperBreakProc.sc   LooperBreakProc : BBCutProc
    classes/LooperCutGate.sc     LooperCutGate   : CutSynth

classes/Looper.sc additionally names 18 BBCut classes (BBCut2, BBCutBuffer,
CutMixer, CutGroup, ExternalClock and the CutBuf/CutMod/CutRev family among
them). That is not an arm's-length dependency: the cutter layer is written to
BBCut's API and is a derivative work of it. A permissive licence on the whole
would have been a claim the code does not support.

BBCut's own version notice is worth recording, because it is the question a
reader will ask. Its COPYING file carries the GPL **version 2** text, but every
source-file notice in the quark reads only:

    "distributed under the terms of the GNU General Public License"

with no version named. GPLv2 section 9 addresses exactly that case:

    "If the Program does not specify a version number of this License, you may
     choose any version ever published by the Free Software Foundation."

So version 3 is a permitted choice here, and GPL-3 is not in conflict with
BBCut. (BBCut.quark likewise records `license: "GPL"`, unversioned.)

No third-party code is redistributed in this repository. Every dependency below
is fetched by the user at install time. The one third-party work in it is the
Bolt Kit's audio, public domain recordings (CC0), below.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Dependencies
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

megalooper carries the same licence as SuperCollider itself: GPL-3.0-or-later.

SuperCollider          GPL-3.0-or-later   https://supercollider.github.io/
  The language, server and class library. Required. 3.14.1 is the minimum:
  Megalooper SA was built and measured on it.

sc3-plugins            GPL-2.0-or-later   https://github.com/supercollider/sc3-plugins
  Required: classes/Looper.sc uses Tartini for pitch detection. Tartini is an SC
  class, not only a server plugin, so the class library will not compile without
  it. Take the release built for your SuperCollider version.

BBCut (nhthn fork)     GPL, unversioned   https://github.com/nhthn/BBCut
  Required: the cutter layer, and a compile-time dependency of the class library.
  Copyright (C) 2001 Nick M. Collins, (C) 2016 Nathan Ho. Archived upstream.
  megalooper is built and tested against commit
  edd86e1cbc4f269828df1acac51afdb3e54bfdb8 (2016-11-12). BBCut's quark file
  names MathLib; megalooper compiles and passes its tests without it.

VSTPlugin 0.6.2        BSD-3-Clause       https://git.iem.at/pd/vstplugin
  Optional: hosts VST2 and VST3 effects in Megalooper SA's chains. Without it
  SA plays its built-in effects and says so. Copyright (C) 2018 Christof Ressi,
  Institute of Electronic Music and Acoustics (IEM), Graz. Its binaries contain
  Steinberg's VST 3 SDK under that SDK's own licence. Never bundled here: you
  install it yourself.

BlackHole 16ch         GPL-3.0            https://existential.audio/blackhole/
  Optional: only the `bh*` routing profiles use it.

AbletonOSC             MIT                https://github.com/ideoforms/AbletonOSC
  Optional: tempo and time-signature push to Ableton Live.

Rogue Amoeba Loopback  commercial         https://rogueamoeba.com/loopback/
  Optional: only the `loopback` routing profile uses it.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Trademarks, and what megalooper took from a Live set
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Ableton and Live are trademarks of Ableton AG. BIAS FX is a trademark of
Positive Grid. VST is a trademark of Steinberg Media Technologies GmbH.
Claude is a trademark of Anthropic, PBC.
megalooper is not affiliated with, sponsored by or endorsed by any of them.
Their names appear only to say what megalooper works with, and where a
setting came from.

Megalooper SA began as a rebuild of the authors' own Live set, and says so
where it matters:

  - Its built-in effects (Pitch Shift, Dual Delay, Envelope Filter, Pitch
    Loops, Tape Echo, Drive, Crusher) are megalooper's own SuperCollider code,
    written to sound like the Live devices that set used. They are
    approximations, not copies, and contain no code of Ableton's.
  - presets/sa/fx-variations.json and presets/sa/rhythmic-steps.json hold
    parameter values read from that set by tools/export-live-set.py: the
    effects' defaults and variations, and the drum patterns.
  - presets/sa/drums/kits/*.json are kit maps: which sample plays on which pad,
    at what level. The kits prefixed core- and the files presets/sa/drums/
    kit-carbon.* and one-hit-kit.* describe drum racks and samples that ship
    with Ableton Live and its packs, by name and path.
  - No audio of Ableton's is in this repository, and megalooper never copies
    any into it: tools/export-live-set.py kits copies samples from your own
    Live installation into a folder git ignores. Without them the drums play
    megalooper's own kit, below.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
The Analog Kit: CC0
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

presets/sa/drums/kits/analog/ and presets/sa/drums/kits/analog.json are
megalooper's own drum kit: sixteen sounds synthesized by
scripts/sa/make-analog-kit.scd in the manner of the classic analog drum
machines, no sample of any machine in them. The sounds and the map are
dedicated to the public domain under CC0 1.0 Universal (the folder's
LICENSE): use them for anything, with no conditions. The script that makes
them is part of megalooper and GPL-3 like the rest.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
The Bolt Kit: CC0
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

presets/sa/drums/kits/bolt/ holds recordings by Karoryfer Samples, from their
libraries Big Rusty Drums and Frankensnare, which their makers dedicated to
the public domain under CC0 1.0 Universal:

  https://github.com/sfzinstruments/karoryfer.big-rusty-drums
  https://github.com/sfzinstruments/karoryfer.frankensnare

scripts/sa/make-bolt-kit.py picks the recordings the kit plays, trims their
silent tails and writes the map, presets/sa/drums/kits/bolt.json. The map and
the trims are dedicated to the public domain under CC0 1.0 as well (the
folder's LICENSE says which commits, and what was changed). The script is part
of megalooper and GPL-3 like the rest.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Reusable without any of the above
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

classes/MeterAnalyzer.sc and classes/PitchAnalyzer.sc are pure analysis: no
BBCut, no sc3-plugins, no server, no GUI. MeterAnalyzer.analyze takes onset
times and strengths and returns a meter and a tempo. If you want the odd-meter
detection and nothing else, those two files are the whole of it, and they carry
the same GPL-3 terms as the rest.
